{"chunk_id": "ca237d84220d", "text": "King Janamejaya asks the sage Vaisampayana to recount the origins and deeds of the Pandavas, who were said to be divine incarnations. The narrative begins with their father, King Pandu, hunting in the forest on the slopes of the Himalayas. Pandu shoots a pair of deer while they are mating, only to discover that the male deer is actually the sage Kindama disguised in animal form. Mortally wounded, the sage rebukes Pandu for killing a creature during intercourse, calling it cruel and unrighteous. In anger and grief, Kindama curses Pandu: whenever Pandu approaches a wife with sexual desire, he will die instantly. This curse becomes a pivotal turning point in the epic, explaining why the Pandavas will later be conceived through divine intervention rather than ordinary union.\nKing Pandu accidentally kills the sage Kindama disguised as a deer during mating and is cursed to die if he approaches his wife sexually.\nThemes: curse and fate, sexual taboo, karmic retribution, origins of the Pandavas, hunting ethics", "vector": [0.008997950702905655, -0.021728385239839554, 0.0016396266873925924, 0.008551386184990406, -0.030499720945954323, 0.02142178826034069, -0.018009232357144356, -0.02330136112868786, -0.016129661351442337, -0.03983093053102493, 0.0014280081959441304, 0.00588532630354166, 0.0029493283946067095, 0.0017879261868074536, -0.0008739676559343934, 0.015863055363297462, 0.04478980228304863, -0.023288030177354813, 0.01682283543050289, -0.02148844115436077, -0.0112241106107831, -0.006538511253893375, -0.007511622738093138, 0.010904183611273766, -0.02644731104373932, 0.03553857281804085, 0.015169879421591759, -0.012370516546070576, -0.012377182021737099, -0.0030343090184032917, -0.02743375301361084, -0.01307702250778675, 0.008444744162261486, -8.222086034948006e-05, -0.029753224924206734, 0.010837532579898834, 0.0008614705293439329, -0.014969924464821815, 0.013536917977035046, -0.02403452806174755, 0.005598725285381079, -0.00671513